  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "The **dot product** multiplies two vectors into a single NUMBER (a scalar):\n\n⟨a, b⟩ · ⟨c, d⟩ = ac + bd.\n\nMultiply matching components, then add. ⟨3, 4⟩ · ⟨1, 2⟩ = 3·1 + 4·2 = 11. The result isn't a vector — it measures how much the two vectors point the same way.",
      "figure": "vec-dot-angle"
    },
    {
      "id": "i1",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "The dot product turns two arrows into one number.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "vectorExplore",
        "mode": "dot",
        "ux": 3,
        "uy": 4,
        "targetDot": 25,
        "vxStart": 1,
        "vyStart": 0,
        "gridMax": 6,
        "prompt": "u = (3, 4). Steer v until the dot product reads 25.",
        "successFeedback": "v = (3, 4) — v equal to u. Then u · v = 3² + 4² = 25, which is |u|² exactly. The dot product of a vector with itself is its length squared, and that is the first clue that this strange number is secretly about geometry.",
        "lowFeedback": "The dot product is below 25 — v is either too short or pointing away from u.",
        "highFeedback": "The dot product is above 25. It maxes out when v points the same way as u and matches its length."
      },
      "predict": {
        "prompt": "Look at the two arrows before you move anything. The dot product u · v of the STARTING pair is…",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "pos",
            "label": "Positive — they lean the same way"
          },
          {
            "id": "neg",
            "label": "Negative — they lean apart"
          },
          {
            "id": "zero",
            "label": "Zero — they're perpendicular"
          }
        ],
        "outcomeId": "pos",
        "reveal": "u · v = 3·1 + 4·0 = 3. One number tells the geometry: positive means the angle is under 90°, zero means exactly 90°, negative means past it."
      },
